       help: this is a supply-chain trust failure, not an ordinary version-
             resolution error. An earlier release carried provenance attestation,
             but @netlify/serverless-functions-api@2.19.1 carries no trust
             evidence.
             
             This can signal a compromised publisher or tampered release. It can
             also be benign release-process drift: a maintainer manually published,
             backported outside the trusted workflow, skipped provenance for
             convenience, or used a registry that stripped metadata.
             
             Before bypassing:
             1. Inspect the package's npm release, source tag/commit, publisher
             identity, and tarball; compare the metadata with npmjs.org, and
             confirm the change is expected and nothing appears tampered with.
             2. Report inconsistent evidence to the relevant upstream owner.
             Package-release drift belongs with the maintainer; metadata present
             on npmjs.org but missing from a proxy or mirror belongs with that
             registry operator.
             3. Only after review, pin a version that retains evidence or add
             the narrow `@netlify/serverless-functions-api@2.19.1` exception to
             `trustPolicyExclude`. A bare `@netlify/serverless-functions-api`
             exempts every version; `trustPolicy = off` disables this protection
             for the entire install.
             
